<p><strong>To choose booking software in Nepal, check that it refuses double-bookings, handles more than one branch, records payments by method and discounts with approval, and gives the owner reports across branches. Ask for a demo with your own services before you decide.</strong></p>

<p>Choosing booking software is a bigger decision than it looks. Your staff will use it every day, your customers will meet it, and your revenue records will live in it. Here is a checklist for comparing options, whether you run a spa, salon, gym, tattoo studio or clinic.</p>
<h2>1. Does it stop double-bookings?</h2>
<p>This is the basic test. Ask how the system stops two bookings for the same person, room or time. A calendar that only displays bookings is not enough; it should refuse clashes.</p>
<h2>2. Does it handle more than one branch?</h2>
<p>Even if you have one branch today, ask what happens at two or three. Can the owner see everything in one place? Can staff move between branches?</p>
<h2>3. Does it handle staff, not just time slots?</h2>
<p>Service businesses book people. The system should know which therapist, stylist, artist or trainer is booked, and their availability.</p>
<h2>4. How does it deal with money?</h2>
<p>Look for payment tracking by method &ndash; cash, card and online &ndash; part-payments, and a daily close that you can trust. Ask how discounts are controlled.</p>
<h2>5. Who can see and change what?</h2>
<p>Owners, managers and front desk need different access. Role-based permissions protect your data and your margins.</p>
<h2>6. Does it fit how you actually work in Nepal?</h2>
<p>Ask about the payment methods you use, the languages your team speaks, and what support looks like locally. Check what is genuinely supported today, not what is promised for later.</p>
<h2>7. How hard is it to start?</h2>
<p>Ask how long onboarding takes, whether your existing bookings can be moved over, and what support you get. Then ask for a demo with your own services and branches.</p>
